From 734d9c551884b88fc53992d10cdbe735d0f93bd1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 18:34:03 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09302/11362] Set the CUDA 13 toolchain AFTER installing vLLM,
 not before it exists

Kernel v29 staged the flashinfer AOT cache correctly and still died in 280 s. The
saved server log -- added in the previous commit for exactly this -- names the cause on
its own: `RuntimeError: No supported CUDA architectures found for major versions [12]`.

An ordering bug, mine. The block that points CUDA_HOME/PATH at the coherent CUDA 13.0.x
toolchain probed for `nvidia/cu13/bin/nvcc` BEFORE the offline pip install ran. That
package arrives WITH the vLLM wheel closure, so the probe could never find it, `_cu` was
None, the env was never set, and flashinfer fell back to the image's CUDA 12 -- which has
no sm_120. A staged kernel cache does not help a toolchain that cannot name the
architecture.

The tell was printed three times before I read it: `cuda_home=None` in v27, v28 and v29's
own backend lines. The AOT dataset was genuinely missing and genuinely needed, so fixing
it was not wrong, but it was not the whole cause and the log said so each run.

The block now runs after a successful install, and says so loudly when `_cu` is still
None, since that is now a known failure signature rather than a silent one.

